Then target = m_2 ^ top53(T(A0))... Hmm, but here's the bug: T(A0) vs T(A0 ^ e). Since T is GF(2)-linear and A0 ^ e = A0 + e (because A0's low 11 bits are 0 and e < 2048, so A0 ^ e = A0 + e), we have T(x_1) = T(A0) ^ T(e) ✓ (GF(2)-linear). And top53(T(x_1)) = top53(T(A0)) ^ top53(T(e)) ✓. And top53(T(e)) = XOR over set bits j of top53(T(1<<j)) ✓. So target = m_2 ^ top53(T(A0)), and we need XOR of basis[j] over set bits j of e = target.

In my code, for the "identity" case, I compute basis[j] = T(1<<j)>>11 ✓, xA = T(A0) ✓, yA = xA (since MULTS[mi]==0) ✓, target = m_2 ^ (yA>>11) ✓.

Then the Gaussian elimination... Let me check the elimination code:

```
uint64_t w2[11]; uint32_t comb[11]; int n2=0; int pv2[11];
for(int i=0;i<11;++i){ w2[i]=b53[i]; comb[i]=1u<<i; }
for(int i=0;i<11;++i){ uint64_t cur=w2[i]; uint32_t cc=comb[i]; int hb;
   while(cur){ hb=63; while(!((cur>>hb)&1)) --hb;
      int k; for(k=0;k<n2;++k) if(pv2[k]==hb){ cur^=w2[k]; cc^=comb[k]; break; }
      if(k==n2) break; }
   if(!cur) continue;
   hb=63; while(!((cur>>hb)&1)) --hb;
   for(int k=0;k<n2;++k) if((w2[k]>>hb)&1){ w2[k]^=cur; comb[k]^=cc; }
   w2[n2]=cur; comb[n2]=cc; pv2[n2]=hb; ++n2;
}
```
